cement for each judge was based on Total Points awarded by that judge to the skaters.  The points were based on 60% for Compulsory Figures and 40% for Free Skating.  Final placement was determined by a Majority Placement rule.  Thus, if a skater was ranked first by a majority of the judges, that skater was placed first overall, and the process was repeated for each place.  If no absolute majority for a place existed, the tiebreakers were, in order: 1) Total Ordinals, 2) Total Points, 3) Compulsory Figure Points."]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #1"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5001324"} "August Anderberg"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SWE.png"}] "SWE"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #2"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/1202424"} "Louis Magnus"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #3"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5002008"} "Eudore Lamborelle"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/BEL"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/BEL.png"}] "BEL"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #4"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/5003129"} "Knut Meinich"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/NOR.png"}] "NOR"]]] [:tr {:class "referees", :style ""} [:th {} "Judge #5"] [:td {:style "width: 400px"} [:a {:href "/athletes/99117"} "Henry Yglesias"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The ladies’ competition was very difficult to handicap.  The scoring varied widely, as witnessed by " [:a {:href "/athletes/80676"} "Theresa Weld"] " receiving two first place votes and one last place vote, and eventual fifth placer " [:a {:href "/athletes/81855"} "Margot Moe"] " of Norway receiving one first place vote and three last place votes. " [:a {:href "/athletes/81426"} "Magda Julin"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/81948"} "Svea Norén"] " won the gold and silver medals, respectively, because of the consistency of their placements.  Magda Julin won the gold medal despite not being placed first by any judge, the only time this has ever happened in the Olympics.  A measure of the anti-German sentiment of the time was evidenced by the choice of music for Magda Julin’s free skating programme.  She had trained to “An der schönen blauen Donau,” a German song, but it was forbidden, and she had to change music at the last minute."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The 1920 Olympic Games was the only time that Magda Julin competed in international skating.  Norén was better known.  At the World Championships, she had been 3rd in 1913, and she would win silver in 1922 and another bronze in 1923.  Bronze medalist Theresa Weld, later Theresa Weld-Blanchard, had the longest career of any of the women in 1920.  She won the 1914 and 1920-1924 U.S. women’s title and, partnered with " [:a {:href "/athletes/95852"} "Nathanial Niles"] ", she was U.S. pairs champion in 1918 and 1920-1927.  Weld-Blanchard was the first North American women’s champion (1923), and she and Niles won the North American pairs title in 1925."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Weld-Blanchard actually deserved the silver medal, and figure skating historian Benjamin Wright has noted, “The day after the ladies event, a re-tabulation of the marks showed that Theresa Weld had in fact earned more points than Svea Norén of Sweden, and deserved the silver medal.  The judges had approximated her points and awarded ordinals and the medals accordingly.  A protest was never made and the results stood as originally announced.  It was a commentary on the fact that the I.S.U. did not participate in conducting the events.”"] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Majority Placements"] [:th {} "Total Ordinals"] [:th {} "Total Points"] [:th {} "Compulsory Figures"] [:th {} "Free Skating"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/81426"} "Magda Julin"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SWE.png"}] "SWE"]] [:td {} "3×2+"] [:td {} "12.0"] [:td {} "913.50"] [:td {} "3×1+ (1)"] [:td {} "5×5+ (4)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {} [:a {:class "footnote", :href "#", :title "\"Olympia-Lexikon\" (Fritz Wasner, 1939) gives completely different point totals for all the ladies in this event. His scores are as follows, listing total first, followed by points for compulsories: Julin – 1,278.90/774.90; Norén – 1,242.85/716.45; Weld – 1,257.20/683.20; Johnson – 1,217.30/713.30; Moe – 1,203.65/699.65; Gulbrandsen – 1,186.50/654.50. It is uncertain how these were derived. The scores listed herein are from \"Lexikon der Olympischen Winter Spiele\" (Erich Kamper, 1972) and corroborate those given in \"Le Vélo Sport\" (newspaper, 1920). These are also the scores in the notes of Christian Schrange (GER).", :data-toggle "tooltip", :data-placement "right"} "1"]]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/81948"} "Svea Norén"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SWE.png"}] "